WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.450
- As petrol and diesel get phased out

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:04.460
and batteries become better and cheaper,

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:07.510
electric trucks are slowly
starting to become a thing.

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:10.510
But the trouble is you need a
massive battery in each truck,

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:12.730
plus the infrastructure
to charge them all.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:16.110
It'd be great if you could
somehow charge up electric trucks

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.610
while they're on the move.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.480
Well, the world already has
a proven, tested solution

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.250
for sending power to massive,
fast-moving vehicles.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.050
Electric trains have pulled
power from overhead wires

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.250
for more than a century.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:31.250
So why not put those wires over
the first lane of a highway

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:34.060
and let trucks connect to it
while they're on the move?

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:36.290
- So it's been running for 21 months now

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:38.720
and will be at least for the next year.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.090
The full-scale scenario for Germany

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:44.980
would be to equip 1/3 of
the German highway network

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:46.350
and that could reduce

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.750
2/3 of the greenhouse gas
emissions by heavy trucks.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:53.250
But for sure, there are pros
and cons in the real world,

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:54.540
and we want to be sure

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.650
before we are going into the
future with such a system.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:00.070
There were a lot of safety
concerns in the beginning,

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:01.640
so we had to discuss everything

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.800
with fire brigade and police and so on.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.060
We have the voltage level of a tram

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:09.620
and the electromagnetic fields
are lower than on a tram,

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.390
so that isn't an issue.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.060
Overtaking is absolutely no problem!

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:16.650
You just set the indicator
or leave the lane

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.810
and the pantograph will
automatically drop down immediately

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:20.790
and they are free to go.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:22.260
So in that part,

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.260
a battery or the combustion
engine will take over,

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:28.310
and you will have the full
power without interruption.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.620
- One of the things that's
really obvious sitting here

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.420
is that the technology works.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:33.860
Like, we're running off grid power now

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.740
and it's really, really quiet in here.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:38.570
There are two main uses
for big trucks like this.

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:40.010
One is last mile delivery,

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:41.720
picking up lots of items from warehouses

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.530
and taking them out to individual shops.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.140
Most of that is going to be away
from the trunk road network,

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.400
so eHighway wouldn't work for that,

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.410
but those trucks tend to
take shorter journeys anyway,

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:51.420
and they're sitting idle at night.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.420
So those could charge at the depot.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.090
The other use for big trucks

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:58.185
is taking containers
from ports to warehouses,

00:01:58.185 --> 00:02:00.690
or from one depot to another.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.810
Almost all of that traffic
goes along big trunk roads like this,

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.870
and that could all be converted
to run straight from the grid

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.230
with batteries taking a
couple of miles at each end.

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:10.140
Roll that out,

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.730
and suddenly you're
replacing a lot of diesel.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:13.563
- What you're doing is

00:02:13.563 --> 00:02:16.810
bringing the electrons to
the vehicle in a cable,

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:19.810
rather than trying to carry
them in a box in a battery.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.210
It's the most efficient solution,

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.680
the most efficient use of energy.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.630
We think that it's possible

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.400
for the system to pay for itself.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.340
It's possible to set an electricity price

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.450
sufficiently cheap for the truck operator

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:36.720
that the lower costs will pay for
the additional costs of the lorry

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.600
in about a year and a half,
something like that.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.620
And we think that it's possible to use

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.000
some of the money that comes
back from electricity sales

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.890
to pay the infrastructure provider

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:47.150
in a way which will make that

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.820
an investment that's
fundable by private finance.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:53.360
And we think there is enough
money left on top of that

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:58.360
to pay a tax to the government
to replace fuel tax.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:02.650
Where you've got real difficulties
is some of these journeys

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:05.030
into really remote parts of the country.

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:06.930
And there's a strong
case for saying, look,

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:10.500
in some of those journeys,
let's have a hybrid vehicle.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.820
But the majority of the
system works very well

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.480
on very efficient electricity,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:16.720
and you can do it quickly.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.720
So I think that's an attractive
thing to do if you can.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.960
- We've had more than a century
to develop delivery infrastructure

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.350
that's based around
combusting fossil fuels,

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:29.350
and that's going to end in
the next couple of decades.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:30.470
Britain is planning to ban

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:33.030
the sale of new petrol
and diesel trucks by 2040,

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:34.760
which sounds like the far future,

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.910
but it's actually only 18 years away.

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:38.740
The rest of Europe won't be far behind

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.870
and building those
wires above enough roads

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:42.720
could take up most of that time,

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.400
if that's the solution that's chosen.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.180
So right now we know the
basic technology does work.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:48.600
That's what's been tested here,

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.930
but a lot of things that technically work
can't be built economically.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.220
If we want to keep to that date,

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:56.190
then the UK has somewhere
around three to five years

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.810
to figure out if this is just a gimmick

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:01.340
or whether it's worth
investing the billions

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.160
that it'll take to roll this out.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.400
Personally, I try not to give
predictions about the future,

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:06.890
but I wouldn't be too surprised

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:09.520
if those wires become part
of everyday life on the road

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.020
in the next couple of decades.

